    "earlier_parts": [
      {
        "question_id": "q-132985",
        "problem": "Find the value of $f(x)$ at $x=4.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(4)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
        "status": "ambiguous",
        "answers": [
          {
            "key": "field-1",
            "correct_value": "8",
            "ma_basis": "none"
          }
        ],
        "feedback": {
          "grade": "Correct",
          "source_grade": "Correct",
          "result": "Correct",
          "worked_solution": "We substitute $4$ for $x$ in the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3\\sqrt{x}+2 \\\\ f(4) & =3\\sqrt{4}+2 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2+2 \\\\  & =6+2 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "solution_evidence": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14209261/observations/history-solution-q-132985-13abddb6326bd545c427.html",
          "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14209261/observations/history-q-132985-a61d615bfee35197040d.html"
        }
      },
      {
        "question_id": "q-132986",
        "problem": "$h(4)\\,=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
        "status": "ambiguous",
        "answers": [
          {
            "key": "field-1",
            "correct_value": "9",
            "ma_basis": "none"
          }
        ],
        "feedback": {
          "grade": "Correct",
          "source_grade": "Correct",
          "result": "Correct",
          "worked_solution": "Since $x=4$ is in the interval $x\\ge 0,$ we use the second branch of the piecewise function, namely $h(x)=2x+1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(4) & =2(4)+1 \\\\  & =8+1 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "solution_evidence": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14209261/observations/history-solution-q-132986-154f45b40cc5cedf66a9.html",
          "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14209261/observations/history-q-132986-67788ecf1c2924e91e01.html"
        }
      },
      {
        "question_id": "q-132987",
        "problem": "What is the root of the function $g(x)?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The root is at $x\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
        "status": "ambiguous",
        "answers": [
          {
            "key": "field-1",
            "correct_value": "3",
            "ma_basis": "none"
          }
        ],
        "feedback": {
          "grade": "Correct",
          "source_grade": "Correct",
          "result": "Correct",
          "worked_solution": "The roots are the solutions to the equation $g(x)=0,$ which we solve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =0 \\\\ 2x-6 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has the single root $x=3.$",
          "solution_evidence": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14209261/observations/history-solution-q-132987-35f3023f65c4a8c12052.html",
          "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14209261/observations/history-q-132987-8fc9359fa6dad8ac7d48.html"
        }
      },
      {
        "question_id": "q-132988",
        "problem": "Using the graph of $y=h(x)$ on the interval $[-3,2],$ find the global maximum value of $h(x)$ on this interval.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${h}_{max}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
        "status": "ambiguous",
        "answers": [
          {
            "key": "field-1",
            "correct_value": "5",
            "ma_basis": "none"
          }
        ],
        "feedback": {
          "grade": "Correct",
          "source_grade": "Correct",
          "result": "Correct",
          "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n* The global maximum of a function is the highest value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n* A global maximum point of a function is a point $(x,y)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $h(x)=5.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14209261/assets/70c48ae2f451dfb8194a0f93aaaec406c36d381a8d85d2565320c5a7860351e7.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the global maximum is ${h}_{max}=5.$ The function reaches this value when $x=2,$ so the global maximum point is $(2,5).$",
          "solution_evidence": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14209261/observations/history-solution-q-132988-2394765b7f95bf41ec36.html",
          "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14209261/observations/history-q-132988-b588b3a9b3bcd16562b6.html"
        }
      },
      {
        "question_id": "q-132989",
        "problem": "Write the expression for the function $(f+g)(x)$ in terms of $x.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $(f+g)(x)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
        "status": "ambiguous",
        "answers": [
          {
            "key": "field-1",
            "correct_value": "3\\sqrt{x}+2x-4",
            "ma_basis": "none"
          }
        ],
        "feedback": {
          "grade": "Correct",
          "source_grade": "Correct",
          "result": "Correct",
          "worked_solution": "We find $(f+g)(x)$ by adding the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(x) & =(3\\sqrt{x}+2)+(2x-6) \\\\  & =2x+3\\sqrt{x}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "solution_evidence": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14209261/observations/history-solution-q-132989-bcf371fffbb5bb7f8dbd.html",
